E.append(tract("tract-uncinate-fasciculus", "Uncinate fasciculus", "association",
 "The uncinate fasciculus is the hook-shaped bundle that curves around the stem of the Sylvian fissure through the limen insulae, connecting the anterior temporal lobe, the temporal pole and the amygdala with the orbitofrontal and ventral prefrontal cortex. It is the pathway that lets emotion, memory and social meaning from the temporal lobe reach the decision-making cortex of the frontal lobe, and it carries proper names and person knowledge from the temporal pole to the inferior frontal gyrus. It is the last bundle to mature, in the third decade, it is damaged in temporal lobe epilepsy and its surgery, in frontotemporal dementia and in head injury, and its disconnection contributes to memory for names, emotional regulation and social conduct.",
 "From the temporal pole, the anterior superior, middle and inferior temporal gyri, the uncus and the amygdala, curving upward and forward through the limen insulae and the ventral extreme capsule (medial to the IFOF), then fanning into the gyrus rectus, the orbital gyri and the inferior frontal gyrus.",
 "It transmits the emotional value and the identity of stimuli from the amygdala and the temporal pole to the orbitofrontal cortex, which uses them to guide choices and social behavior, and returns frontal regulation to the amygdala; on the left it carries proper-name retrieval and the semantic-frontal connection for naming people, and it contributes to episodic memory encoding by linking the temporal memory system with the frontal strategic system. Bilateral damage or degeneration produces impaired social and emotional judgement, disinhibition and poor name retrieval, the picture of behavioral variant frontotemporal dementia, and its left-sided lesion after temporal lobectomy impairs naming of famous people and memory.",
 ["artery-mca", "artery-mca-inferior-division", "artery-aca", "artery-anterior-choroidal"],
 [mview("sagittal", "tract-uncinate-fasciculus-l", "uncinate fasciculus hooking through the limen insulae"), mview("coronal", "tract-uncinate-fasciculus-r", "uncinate fibers between the temporal pole and the orbitofrontal cortex")],
 "Tractography shows a hook-shaped bundle from the anterior temporal lobe around the Sylvian stem into the orbitofrontal cortex; on coronal T2 the limen insulae, where it passes, is the narrow white-matter bridge between the temporal and frontal lobes.",
 [pathol("Behavioral variant frontotemporal dementia", "MRI", "Orbitofrontal and anterior temporal atrophy with reduced fractional anisotropy of the uncinate, correlating with disinhibition and loss of empathy.", sequence="T1"),
  pathol("Temporal lobe epilepsy and anterior temporal lobectomy", "MRI", "Reduced uncinate integrity ipsilateral to the seizure focus and transection during resection, with impaired famous-face naming.", sequence="FLAIR"),
  pathol("Traumatic brain injury", "MRI", "Shear injury of the uncinate at the limen insulae from orbitofrontal and temporal pole contusions, contributing to personality change.", sequence="SWI")],
 [("Impaired social and emotional decision making, disinhibition", "bilateral", "Disconnection of the amygdala and temporal pole from the orbitofrontal cortex"), ("Impaired retrieval of proper names and person knowledge (left)", "n/a", "Disconnection of the temporal pole from the inferior frontal gyrus"), ("Episodic memory encoding deficits", "bilateral", "Loss of the temporo-frontal memory strategy link")],
 ["Famous face naming and identification", "Social cognition tests and collateral history of conduct", "Delayed recall"],
 ["The uncinate is the last tract to myelinate, which may be why adolescents and their orbitofrontal cortex take so long to agree", "Naming famous people fails after left temporal surgery because the uncinate is cut at the limen", "Frontotemporal dementia is a disease of the uncinate as much as of the cortex at its two ends"],
 [S(7, 262, 268), S(9, 301, 306), B(7, 61, 62), B(22, 236, 238), B(18, 169, 172)], synonyms=["fasciculus uncinatus", "temporo-orbitofrontal bundle"], mesh_ids=T("tract-uncinate-fasciculus"), pathways=["pathway-papez-limbic"], syndromes=["syn-kluver-bucy", "syn-anosmia"],
 territories=["territory-mca-temporal", "territory-mca-insular", "territory-aca"],
 afferents=[("Temporal pole, anterior temporal gyri, uncus and amygdala", "temporal origin")],
 efferents=[("Orbitofrontal cortex, gyrus rectus and inferior frontal gyrus", "frontal termination")]))

E.append(tract("tract-cingulum-frontoparietal", "Cingulum, fronto-parietal", "limbic",
 "The dorsal part of the cingulum bundle runs in the white matter of the cingulate gyrus above the corpus callosum, from the subgenual and anterior cingulate cortex back to the posterior cingulate and the precuneus, collecting and distributing fibers along its length. It is the backbone of the medial default mode and limbic networks, connecting the anterior thalamic radiation and the medial prefrontal cortex with the posterior cingulate, retrosplenial cortex and parietal cortex, and it carries the fibers that cingulotomy divides for pain and obsessive-compulsive disorder. Its integrity is reduced in Alzheimer disease, mild cognitive impairment and depression, and its interruption by anterior cerebral artery infarcts, butterfly gliomas and callosal surgery produces attention, memory and motivational deficits.",
 "Within the cingulate gyrus from the subcallosal region forward of the genu, over the body of the corpus callosum in the depth of the cingulate gyrus, to the splenium, where it continues as the parahippocampal cingulum; it receives and gives off fibers to the medial frontal, cingulate, precuneal and parietal cortex along the way.",
 "The dorsal cingulum links the anterior cingulate and medial prefrontal cortex with the posterior cingulate, precuneus and retrosplenial cortex, carrying the executive, motivational and attentional signals of the medial frontal lobe to the posterior memory and self-referential regions and returning memory retrieval signals forward; it also carries the anterior thalamic projection to the cingulate cortex and the cingulate's input to the motor and prefrontal cortex. Interruption reduces attention and executive function, disturbs the affective component of pain (the target of cingulotomy), and disconnects memory retrieval from the frontal strategy, and its degeneration parallels the posterior cingulate hypometabolism of early Alzheimer disease.",
 ["artery-aca", "artery-pca"],
 [mview("sagittal", "tract-cingulum-frontoparietal-l", "dorsal cingulum in the cingulate gyrus above the corpus callosum"), mview("coronal", "tract-cingulum-frontoparietal-r", "cingulum bundle in the depth of the cingulate gyrus")],
 "Tractography shows a long green bundle above the corpus callosum from the subgenual region to the splenium; on coronal T2 it is the round white-matter core of the cingulate gyrus above the callosum.",
 [pathol("Anterior cerebral artery infarct", "MRI", "Restricted diffusion of the medial frontal cortex and the cingulum with apathy, memory impairment and attentional deficit.", sequence="DWI"),
  pathol("Butterfly glioma of the corpus callosum", "MRI", "Enhancing mass crossing the callosum and infiltrating both cingula with personality change and abulia.", sequence="T1+Gd"),
  pathol("Alzheimer disease", "MRI", "Reduced fractional anisotropy of the posterior cingulum on diffusion imaging in mild cognitive impairment, preceding hippocampal atrophy.", sequence="n/a")],
 [("Impaired attention and executive control", "bilateral", "Disconnection of the anterior cingulate from the posterior default mode and parietal cortex"), ("Reduced affective response to pain after cingulotomy", "bilateral", "Interruption of the cingulate limb of the pain network"), ("Memory retrieval deficits", "bilateral", "Disconnection of the frontal strategy from the posterior cingulate retrieval hub")],
 ["Sustained attention and executive testing", "Delayed recall with cues", "Mood and motivation"],
 ["The cingulum is the medial highway of the limbic lobe; Papez sends memory along it from the anterior thalamus to the parahippocampal gyrus", "Cingulotomy leaves the sensation and removes the suffering by cutting this bundle", "Posterior cingulum degeneration is one of the earliest white-matter changes of Alzheimer disease"],
 [S(9, 301, 306), S(7, 262, 268), B(7, 61, 62), B(22, 233, 236)], synonyms=["dorsal cingulum", "cingulate bundle"], mesh_ids=T("tract-cingulum-frontoparietal"), pathways=["pathway-papez-limbic"], syndromes=["syn-aca-infarct", "syn-callosal-disconnection"],
 territories=["territory-aca", "territory-pca-occipital"], parent="gyrus-cingulate",
 afferents=[("Anterior thalamic nuclei and medial prefrontal cortex", "anterior origin"), ("Posterior cingulate, precuneus and parietal cortex", "posterior origin")],
 efferents=[("Cingulate cortex along its length", "distributed termination"), ("Parahippocampal cingulum and entorhinal cortex", "posterior continuation")]))

E.append(tract("tract-cingulum-parahippocampal", "Cingulum, parahippocampal", "limbic",
 "The parahippocampal cingulum is the ventral continuation of the cingulum bundle below the splenium, running through the isthmus into the white matter of the parahippocampal gyrus toward the entorhinal cortex and the temporal pole. It closes the Papez circuit by carrying the anterior thalamic and posterior cingulate output to the entorhinal cortex, and it links the retrosplenial navigation cortex with the parahippocampal place area, so it serves both memory and spatial orientation. It is the medial temporal white matter that atrophies with the hippocampus in Alzheimer disease and mesial temporal sclerosis, and it is interrupted by PCA infarcts, temporal lobectomy and posterior medial temporal tumors, producing amnesia and topographical disorientation.",
 "From the retrosplenial cortex behind and below the splenium, through the isthmus of the cingulate gyrus, downward and forward in the white matter of the parahippocampal gyrus medial to the collateral sulcus and lateral to the hippocampus, to the entorhinal cortex, the perirhinal cortex and the temporal pole.",
 "It conveys the posterior cingulate and retrosplenial cortex's spatial and self-referential signals and the anterior thalamic memory signal to the entorhinal cortex, the gateway of the hippocampus, and returns hippocampal-entorhinal output to the posterior cingulate, forming the loop through which episodes are encoded with their spatial context and retrieved; the retrosplenial-parahippocampal link converts the allocentric map into the egocentric view used for navigation. Interruption produces anterograde amnesia with prominent disorientation in space, and reduced integrity of the bundle is a marker of medial temporal degeneration that correlates with memory scores in mild cognitive impairment and Alzheimer disease.",
 ["artery-pca", "artery-anterior-choroidal"],
 [mview("sagittal", "tract-cingulum-parahippocampal-l", "parahippocampal cingulum from the isthmus into the parahippocampal gyrus"), mview("coronal", "tract-cingulum-parahippocampal-r", "cingulum in the white matter of the parahippocampal gyrus")],
 "Tractography shows the cingulum curving down behind the splenium and forward in the parahippocampal gyrus; on coronal T2 it is the white matter of the parahippocampal gyrus beneath the hippocampus.",
 [pathol("Alzheimer disease", "MRI", "Medial temporal atrophy with reduced parahippocampal white matter and cingulum integrity, correlating with memory impairment.", sequence="T1"),
  pathol("PCA infarct with amnesia", "MRI", "Restricted diffusion of the medial temporal lobe and parahippocampal white matter, often with a hemianopia; bilateral lesions leave permanent amnesia.", sequence="DWI"),
  pathol("Mesial temporal sclerosis", "MRI", "Hippocampal atrophy with ipsilateral parahippocampal white-matter loss and reduced cingulum anisotropy.", sequence="FLAIR")],
 [("Anterograde amnesia", "bilateral", "Interruption of the Papez circuit at its entorhinal limb"), ("Topographical disorientation", "n/a", "Disconnection of the retrosplenial navigation cortex from the parahippocampal place system"), ("Impaired contextual memory", "bilateral", "Loss of the posterior cingulate-entorhinal link that binds events to places")],
 ["Delayed recall and recognition", "Route finding and map drawing", "Visual fields when a PCA lesion is suspected"],
 ["The Papez circuit exits the cingulate through this bundle to reach the hippocampus", "Getting lost precedes forgetting in some patients with early Alzheimer disease: the parahippocampal cingulum goes first", "A posterior medial temporal lesion that spares the hippocampus can still cause amnesia by cutting this bundle"],
 [S(9, 301, 306), S(7, 262, 268), B(7, 61, 62), B(22, 233, 236)], synonyms=["ventral cingulum", "parahippocampal white matter bundle"], mesh_ids=T("tract-cingulum-parahippocampal"), pathways=["pathway-papez-limbic"], syndromes=["syn-wernicke-korsakoff", "syn-pca-infarct"],
 territories=["territory-pca-temporal"], parent="gyrus-parahippocampal-uncus",
 afferents=[("Retrosplenial and posterior cingulate cortex, anterior thalamus", "posterior origin")],
 efferents=[("Entorhinal and perirhinal cortex, temporal pole", "anterior termination")]))

E.append(tract("tract-dentatorubrothalamic", "Dentatorubrothalamic tract", "cerebellar",
 "The dentatorubrothalamic tract is the main outflow of the cerebellum, from the dentate nucleus through the superior cerebellar peduncle, crossing in the decussation of the peduncles in the caudal midbrain, passing through and around the contralateral red nucleus, and ending in the ventrolateral thalamus, from which the motor cortex is reached. It is the pathway through which the cerebellum times and coordinates voluntary movement, and it is the reason cerebellar signs are ipsilateral: it crosses once here and the corticospinal tract crosses back. Its interruption produces contralateral (after the decussation) or ipsilateral (before it) ataxia and tremor, its lesion in the midbrain gives the Holmes rubral tremor, and it is the bundle that tremor surgery targets in the thalamus and the posterior subthalamic area.",
 "From the dentate nucleus in the cerebellar white matter, forward and upward in the superior cerebellar peduncle along the roof of the fourth ventricle, decussating in the tegmentum at the level of the inferior colliculus, ascending through and around the red nucleus and the prerubral field, through the zona incerta and field H of Forel, to the ventral intermediate and ventrolateral nuclei of the thalamus.",
 "The tract carries the dentate nucleus's prediction-corrected motor signal, derived from the cerebellar hemisphere's comparison of intended and actual movement, to the motor and premotor cortex through the ventrolateral thalamus, giving movements their timing, smoothness and end-point accuracy; collaterals to the red nucleus feed the rubrospinal and the rubro-olivary loop for motor learning, and its rhythmic activity is what drives cerebellar and Holmes tremor. Because it crosses in the midbrain, a lesion of the peduncle or the dentate produces ipsilateral ataxia, a lesion above the decussation contralateral ataxia; combined damage to the tract and the nigrostriatal fibers in the midbrain produces the slow, coarse rest-postural-intention tremor of Holmes.",
 ["artery-sca", "artery-pca", "arteries-thalamoperforating", "artery-basilar"],
 [mview("coronal", "tract-dentatorubrothalamic-l", "dentatorubrothalamic tract from the dentate nucleus through the red nucleus to the thalamus"), mview("axial", "tract-dentatorubrothalamic-r", "superior cerebellar peduncle decussation in the caudal midbrain")],
 "Tractography shows a bundle from the dentate through the superior cerebellar peduncle, crossing in the midbrain to the contralateral thalamus; the decussation is a dark midline structure on axial T2 at the inferior colliculus level, and the tract is used to plan tremor stimulation.",
 [pathol("Holmes (rubral) tremor after midbrain lesion", "MRI", "Infarct, hemorrhage or demyelination of the midbrain tegmentum involving the red nucleus and the tract, with delayed coarse tremor at rest, posture and action.", sequence="FLAIR", timing="Tremor appears weeks to months after the lesion"),
  pathol("Superior cerebellar peduncle lesion (SCA infarct, MS)", "MRI", "Lesion of the peduncle with ipsilateral limb ataxia and intention tremor.", sequence="DWI"),
  pathol("Ventral intermediate thalamic stimulation", "MRI", "Electrode at the thalamic termination of the tract, planned by tractography, for essential and parkinsonian tremor.", sequence="T1")],
 [("Ipsilateral ataxia and intention tremor (lesion below the decussation)", "ipsilateral", "Interruption before the crossing"), ("Contralateral ataxia and tremor (lesion above the decussation)", "contralateral", "Interruption after the crossing"), ("Holmes tremor", "contralateral", "Combined damage of the cerebellothalamic and nigrostriatal pathways in the midbrain")],
 ["Finger-nose test with attention to tremor at the end of movement", "Compare rest, postural and intention components", "Side of ataxia relative to the lesion level on imaging"],
 ["Cerebellar signs are ipsilateral because this tract crosses once and the corticospinal tract crosses back", "A tremor that is present at rest, on posture and on action after a midbrain stroke is Holmes tremor", "Tremor surgery follows this bundle: the target is where it enters the thalamus"],
 [S(6, 236, 242), S(5, 212, 218), S(12, 364, 370), B(8, 67, 73), B(23, 246, 250)], synonyms=["cerebellothalamic tract", "superior cerebellar peduncle outflow"], mesh_ids=T("tract-dentatorubrothalamic"), pathways=["pathway-cerebellar-circuits", "pathway-rubrospinal"], syndromes=["syn-claude", "syn-benedikt", "syn-cerebellar-hemispheric", "syn-sca-infarct"],
 territories=["territory-superior-cerebellar", "territory-basilar", "territory-posterior-choroidal-thalamoperforating"], parent="superior-cerebellar-peduncle",
 afferents=[("Dentate and interposed nuclei", "origin")],
 efferents=[("Contralateral red nucleus", "collateral termination"), ("Contralateral ventrolateral thalamus (ventral intermediate nucleus)", "termination")]))

E.append(tract("tract-reticulospinal", "Reticulospinal tract", "projection",
 "The reticulospinal tract descends from the pontine and medullary reticular formation to the spinal cord, bilaterally with an ipsilateral predominance, in the anterior and lateral funiculi, reconstructed here from its brainstem origin down to the cervicomedullary junction. It is the main non-pyramidal motor pathway for posture, locomotion and axial and proximal movement: the pontine (medial) component facilitates extensor tone and the medullary (lateral) component inhibits it and gates reflexes, and both carry the cortex's commands for gross movement, the startle response and the autonomic outflow to the intermediolateral column. It is what keeps a patient with a corticospinal lesion able to stand and walk, and its imbalance after such lesions produces spasticity; its loss below the lesion in a complete cord injury produces flaccidity and autonomic failure.",
 "From the pontine reticular formation (nucleus reticularis pontis oralis and caudalis) mainly ipsilaterally in the anterior funiculus (medial reticulospinal tract) and from the medullary gigantocellular reticular formation bilaterally in the lateral funiculus (lateral reticulospinal tract), descending the whole length of the cord to the ventromedial gray of the ventral horn and the intermediolateral column.",
 "The pontine reticulospinal fibers excite axial and proximal extensor motor neurons and set the postural tone that supports the body against gravity, and the medullary fibers inhibit them and dampen stretch reflexes; the cortex controls both through corticoreticular fibers that run with the corticospinal tract, so a capsular lesion removes cortical drive to the inhibitory medullary system and leaves the pontine facilitatory system unopposed, producing spasticity. The tract also carries the locomotor commands from the mesencephalic locomotor region and the pedunculopontine nucleus that generate stepping, the startle reflex, and descending sympathetic and respiratory control. It underlies the recovery of gross movement and gait after stroke and the abnormal postures of decerebrate rigidity.",
 ["artery-basilar", "arteries-pontine", "artery-vertebral", "anterior-spinal-artery"],
 [mview("sagittal", "tract-reticulospinal-l", "reticulospinal fibers descending from the pontomedullary reticular formation"), mview("axial", "tract-reticulospinal-r", "reticulospinal tract in the ventral brainstem tegmentum")],
 "Tractography shows fibers from the pontomedullary tegmentum descending to the cervicomedullary junction, medial and ventral to the corticospinal bundle; they are not visible on conventional MRI.",
 [pathol("Spasticity after capsular stroke (functional)", "MRI", "The corticospinal and corticoreticular lesion in the posterior limb; spasticity develops over weeks as the unopposed pontine reticulospinal drive reorganizes.", sequence="FLAIR", timing="Spasticity emerges 1-6 weeks after the lesion"),
  pathol("Decerebrate posturing from midbrain-pontine damage", "CT", "Central herniation or pontine hemorrhage disconnecting the pontine reticulospinal system from higher control, with extensor posturing of all limbs.", sequence="CT non-contrast"),
  pathol("Hereditary hyperekplexia and exaggerated startle", "EEG", "Excessive startle from reticulospinal hyperexcitability without a structural lesion; glycine receptor mutations.", sequence="n/a")],
 [("Spasticity and hyperreflexia after upper motor neuron lesions", "contralateral", "Loss of cortical drive to the inhibitory medullary reticulospinal system"), ("Decerebrate rigidity", "bilateral", "Unopposed pontine reticulospinal and vestibulospinal facilitation after midbrain transection"), ("Loss of postural tone, gait and autonomic control in cord transection", "bilateral", "Interruption of both reticulospinal components below the lesion")],
 ["Tone in the limbs and its velocity dependence", "Posturing pattern in coma: flexor (decorticate) versus extensor (decerebrate)", "Gait and trunk control in patients with corticospinal lesions"],
 ["The corticospinal tract makes fingers move; the reticulospinal tract makes the body stand up", "Spasticity is not a corticospinal sign but a corticoreticular one", "Stroke patients walk again because the reticulospinal tract, not the pyramidal tract, carries the legs"],
 [S(4, 152, 166), S(9, 299, 301), S(5, 199, 212), B(4, 33, 38), B(9, 75, 82)], synonyms=["medial and lateral reticulospinal tracts", "pontine and medullary reticulospinal tracts"], mesh_ids=T("tract-reticulospinal"), pathways=["pathway-reticulospinal", "pathway-vestibulospinal"], syndromes=["syn-complete-transection-spinal-shock", "syn-central-transtentorial-herniation", "syn-lacunar-pure-motor"],
 territories=["territory-basilar"], parent="reticular-formation",
 afferents=[("Pontine and medullary reticular formation", "origin"), ("Motor and premotor cortex (corticoreticular fibers)", "input to the reticular formation")],
 efferents=[("Ventral horn interneurons and axial motor neurons", "termination"), ("Intermediolateral column (sympathetic)", "termination")]))

